34959	{"artifactId":"DEF-b7a68147dcf72034","entry":{"id":"review-18dc035ab800b0003fbe3d31e42257f8","actor":{"id":"insrc:review","kind":"system","properties":{}},"timestamp":"2026-10-23T08:45:55.619Z","operation":"review","outcome":{"result":"block","summary":"review: block","data":{"kind":"review","report":{"artifact":"define","stage":"define","verdict":"block","findings":[{"claimId":"p1","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"The tool executor auto-approves tools that declare requiresApproval (true or a predicate), logging a warning and running the tool without asking anyone.","evidence":"src/daemon/tools/executor.ts:44 logs `log.warn({ id: tool.id }, 'tool requires approval but the gate dispatcher is gone; auto-approving')`, so the executor does warn and auto-approve tools that need a gate. The read of executor.ts:41 is cut off at `const needsGate = tool.requiresApproval === true`. It does not show whether a predicate-form `requiresApproval` (docs/plans/tools.md:57 `boolean | ((input: unknown) => boolean)`) is evaluated and counted toward `needsGate`. It also does not show whether line 44 runs on every call or only on a branch where the dispatcher is missing. The evidence supports the auto-approve-with-warning behaviour for `requiresApproval: true`. The 'or a predicate' part and the 'always, without asking anyone' part are not confirmed.","action":"Read src/daemon/tools/executor.ts:38-50 to confirm two things: that the full `needsGate` expression evaluates predicate-form requiresApproval, and that the auto-approve branch runs every time and is not a fallback. Then reword the premise to cite executor.ts:41-44 and to state the condition it runs under ('the gate dispatcher is gone').","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The core claim holds: the executor warns and auto-approves a tool that requires approval (executor.ts:44). Whether this covers predicates, and whether it happens on every call, is not shown by the evidence. The work prescribed by the premise (a uniform approval rule in place of the auto-approve) most likely still applies, so this is imprecision and not a build-breaking contradiction.","options":["Keep the premise and add the anchor 'executor.ts:41-44 (the gate dispatcher is gone, so every gated call is auto-approved with a warning)' once the read confirms the branch runs every time","Narrow the premise to `requiresApproval === true` if the full line 41 shows predicates are not evaluated","Reword it as a conditional fallback ('when no gate dispatcher is registered, gated tools are auto-approved with a warning') if line 44 is in a fallback branch"]}},{"claimId":"p2","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"The executor has a single entry point executeTool(toolId, input, deps) that every registry tool call passes through, so a hook stage and result-shape check can be hosted there.","evidence":"The entry point exists: `src/daemon/tools/executor.ts:23: export async function executeTool(` (grep for /export async function executeTool/ found 1 match, and the read of line 23 found it). A grep for /executeTool\\(/ found only one call site in source: `src/agent/read-only-tool-loop.ts:78`, which calls `executeTool(name, input as never, deps)`. That line also accepts an injectable `args.execute` override, so this caller can be routed around executeTool. The third match is a measurement JSON under docs/, which is not code. Nothing gathered covers the rest of the claim: no search was run for other ways a registry tool can be called, such as code that calls a tool's handler directly, IPC tool handlers in daemon/index.ts, MCP paths or workflow steps. So 'every registry tool call passes through' is neither confirmed nor contradicted. The evidence confirms the function and its (name, input, deps) signature. It does not show that the function is the only route.","action":"Before hosting the hook stage and the result-shape check in executeTool, check that no path invokes a registry tool without going through it. Search for direct handler calls (e.g. `.handler(`, `.execute(`, `registry.get(`) under src/daemon, src/mcp and src/workflow. Also note that read-only-tool-loop.ts:78 accepts an injectable `args.execute` that skips executeTool. Either make that override go through the hook stage too, or say in the artifact that it is exempt (for example, test-only). If any direct paths exist, route them through executeTool or reword the premise to list them.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The anchor is correct, but the evidence cannot show the claim that every call passes through executeTool. The one confirmed caller also has an injectable override that bypasses it. A person needs to decide whether to widen the scope or reword the claim.","options":["Keep the premise, and add a task that checks for and routes any direct tool-handler calls through executeTool, including the `args.execute` override in read-only-tool-loop.ts:78.","Reword the premise to: 'executeTool(toolId, input, deps) at src/daemon/tools/executor.ts:23 is the standard entry point for registry tool calls (the caller found is src/agent/read-only-tool-loop.ts:78); injected executors and any direct handler calls must also be routed through the hook stage.'","Scope the hook stage to calls that go through executeTool only, and document which paths are exempt."]}},{"claimId":"p3","kind":"inventory","severity":"LOW","premise":"The workflow library, the daemon and the terminal client's approval service run git/gh directly through their own wrappers in more than twenty places.","evidence":"The evidence supports the count. The `runGit(` grep finds 23 production call sites in the workflow library. They are in src/workflow/team: anchor-finder.ts:37,40; handoff.ts:288,312,422,423,624,708,732; policy-reader.ts:68,80,94,116,137,140,161,165; policy-refresh.ts:54; sighting-store.ts:78; and signoff.ts:388,395,569. The wrapper itself is defined at src/workflow/team/git.ts:25. The `commitAndPushArtifacts(` grep, whose wrapper is defined at src/workflow/tracker/github.ts:120, finds 5 more production callers. Two are in the terminal client's approval service, at src/cli/services/workflow.ts:139 and :174. The others are src/workflow/questions.ts:470, src/workflow/resolve-comment.ts:218 and src/workflow/team/signoff.ts:385. That makes 28 or more direct git/gh call sites through the library's own wrappers, which is well over twenty. One part is not shown. The gathered matches under src/daemon are all in tests, such as build-step.test.ts and repo-settings.test.ts. The execFileSync grep was cut off at 50 matches, and the only daemon production call it surfaces is quoted in an ISSUE doc (validation-checks.ts:323-338). So the daemon's share is not shown directly. This does not change the outcome, because the workflow library and the CLI already pass twenty.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The count holds: 23 runGit call sites plus 5 commitAndPushArtifacts call sites, 28 in all. The daemon's share is not shown in this evidence, but the threshold is met without it, so no change is needed."}},{"claimId":"p4","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"runGit is a direct git shell-out wrapper defined in the workflow library.","evidence":"grep /export function runGit/ found exactly one source match, at src/workflow/team/git.ts:25: `export function runGit(cwd: string, args: readonly string[], timeoutMs: number = LOCAL_GIT_TIMEOUT_MS): GitRun {`. Reading src/workflow/team/git.ts:25 returns the same line. The other two matches are in DEF.md (docs/epics/.../DEF.md:230 and :240), and both agree that runGit is defined at this anchor. So the anchor resolves to runGit in the workflow library (src/workflow/team/). It takes a cwd, git args and a timeout, which fits a git shell-out wrapper. The evidence does not show the function body, so the exact spawn mechanism is not visible here. The signature and the name are enough to support the premise.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The premise is verified at the cited anchor, so no change is needed."}},{"claimId":"p5","kind":"citation","severity":"MED","premise":"commitAndPushArtifacts is a direct git shell-out defined in src/workflow/tracker/github.ts, which itself contains gh shell-outs.","evidence":"The location is confirmed: grep /export function commitAndPushArtifacts/ finds the source definition at src/workflow/tracker/github.ts:120, and the read of that line returns `export function commitAndPushArtifacts(repoPath: string, paths: readonly string[], message: string): CommitArtifactsResult {`. The evidence does not show the function body, so nothing here proves it shells out to git directly. The /['\"]gh['\"]/ grep stopped at its 50-match cap before it reached any source line in github.ts, so the file's gh shell-outs are only backed by committed documents, not by source lines: docs/epics/team-signoff-claim-based-workflow-E20261016315d510c/S001/T010-REGRESSION.md:16 says every out(/silent( call with a 'gh' or 'git' command in non-test src is in src/workflow/tracker/github.ts, and S009/LLD.md:406 cites `silent('gh', ['auth','status'])` at github.ts:168-175. The premise is very likely true, but this evidence does not verify its main point (commitAndPushArtifacts is a direct git shell-out).","action":"Re-probe the function body, for example by reading src/workflow/tracker/github.ts:120-160 or grepping /silent\\('git'|out\\('git'/ in github.ts only, to confirm that commitAndPushArtifacts runs git through out/silent (the legacy exec). Re-run the gh grep with the path limited to github.ts so source lines, not docs, ground the gh shell-out claim. If the body turns out to call a wrapper rather than git itself, reword the premise to match.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The anchor is correct. What the evidence cannot show is how the shell-out works (direct git versus a wrapper), so the premise is unverified, not contradicted. A narrower probe or a softer wording settles it.","options":["Keep the premise as written and add a probe that reads src/workflow/tracker/github.ts:120-160 so the git shell-out is grounded.","Reword it to: 'commitAndPushArtifacts (src/workflow/tracker/github.ts:120) commits and pushes artifacts. The same module holds the tracker's direct gh/git shell-outs (out/silent).' This keeps the claim to what the cited docs support.","Limit the gh grep to src/workflow/tracker/github.ts so source lines, not docs, ground the module-level claim."]}},{"claimId":"p6","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"The terminal client's approval service runs git or gh itself rather than through the registry.","evidence":"No source line from src/cli/services/workflow.ts is in the evidence. The grep hit its 50-match cap on docs and measurement files and never reached that file. The premise is supported only second-hand, by committed review documents. docs/epics/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E2026102372024208/DEF.md:229 says a grep found production callers of `commitAndPushArtifacts(` at src/cli/services/workflow.ts:139 (`const result = commitAndPushArtifacts(rep…`). DEF.md:254 adds that removing commitAndPushArtifacts would make tsc fail at src/cli/services/workflow.ts:139 and :174. docs/epics/one-common-model-every-artifact-E202610192f27754d/S006/CR.md:34 says approve() at src/cli/services/workflow.ts:147 runs commitApprovedArtifacts and attachApprovedDocuments, and that the second of these calls commitAndPushArtifacts. Separately, DEF.md:231 and S001/T010-REGRESSION.md:20 put commitAndPushArtifacts at src/workflow/tracker/github.ts:120 and describe it as a git commit and push through the out/silent shell-out runners. Taken together, the documents suggest the CLI approval service reaches git through the workflow library's commitAndPushArtifacts wrapper rather than through the registry. That is a path outside the registry, but the service does not call git itself. Neither the call site nor any gh use in workflow.ts is confirmed by a source read.","action":"Re-probe the anchor directly, for example with grep /commitAndPushArtifacts|openTracker|attachApprovedDocument/ restricted to src/cli/services/workflow.ts, plus reads of lines 139, 147 and 174. That confirms the call sites. Then reword the premise: the service runs git (and possibly gh, through attachApprovedDocuments/openTracker) by way of the workflow library's direct shell-out wrappers, not the registry. Keep src/cli/services/workflow.ts in the cutover scope so that removing commitAndPushArtifacts does not break tsc.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"Committed reviews point to commitAndPushArtifacts calls at workflow.ts:139/174, which support the gist of the premise. The evidence holds no source read, though, and it shows the git use is indirect, through the github.ts wrapper. Either the wording or the grounding needs a human-approved adjustment.","options":["Reword: \"The terminal client's approval service (src/cli/services/workflow.ts) runs git and gh through the workflow library's direct shell-out wrappers (commitAndPushArtifacts, the tracker attach path) rather than through the registry.\"","Keep the wording, and add direct file:line anchors (src/cli/services/workflow.ts:139, :174) after re-probing the source.","Narrow the premise to the git commit/push path only (commitAndPushArtifacts) if a re-probe shows no gh use from this service."]}},{"claimId":"p7","kind":"inventory","severity":"LOW","premise":"The registry has no Jira tools today.","evidence":"The case-insensitive grep /(?i)jira/ over the anchor scope (src/daemon/tools/builtins) found 0 matches, so no Jira tool is defined there. The /\\bacli\\b/ grep found 27 matches. Every one is in a planning document: docs/epics/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E2026102372024208/DEF.md (:33, :72, :79, :173, :183, :184, :187, :234, :242, :249, :280, :281), docs/epics/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E20261023b7a68147/DEF.md (:33, :62, :74, :82, :182, :192, :193, :196, :197, :227) and docs/standalone/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E202610230b8e0235/SPEC.md (:9, :13, :22, :56, :62). None is in source under src/daemon/tools/builtins. The evidence supports the claim that the registry has no Jira tools today.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"p8","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"Registry tools return loosely typed results (ToolResult with output text/format/success) and declare no result schema.","evidence":"The anchor exists. grep /interface ToolResult/ matches src/daemon/tools/types.ts:33 `export interface ToolResult {` (there is a second ToolResult at src/shared/types.ts:46, and docs/plans/tools.md:78 describes it). The 'declares no result schema' half is supported: none of the 34 /output(Schema|Shape)|resultSchema/ matches is under src/daemon/tools. They are in the analyze planner templates (src/analyze/planner/templates/*), src/shared/analyze-types.ts:123, src/shared/understanding.ts:60 and docs. The evidence does not include the body of ToolResult, so the claimed fields (output text / format / success) and the 'loosely typed' description cannot be checked. Two interfaces share the name (src/daemon/tools/types.ts:33 and src/shared/types.ts:46), so it is also unclear which one registry tools actually return.","action":"Read src/daemon/tools/types.ts:33 onward and confirm that ToolResult has the stated fields (output text, format, success) with no typed payload. Also say which ToolResult the registry executor returns, src/daemon/tools/types.ts or src/shared/types.ts:46. The 'no result schema in the tool registry' part needs no change.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The missing-schema claim is grounded. The field list and which interface is meant are not shown in the evidence, so a person should confirm them before an edit; no edit can be derived safely.","options":["Keep the premise once a read of src/daemon/tools/types.ts:33 confirms the fields output/format/success","Reword the premise to cite the field names and the exact ToolResult it means (src/daemon/tools/types.ts:33 rather than src/shared/types.ts:46)","Narrow the premise to the grounded part: 'no result/output schema is declared anywhere under src/daemon/tools'"]}},{"claimId":"p9","ref":"s4/ac4","kind":"inventory","severity":"LOW","premise":"Existing git tool ids git_pull, git_push, git_reset and git:cherry-pick are registered with mixed naming.","evidence":"All four anchors resolve to the cited ids: src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/pull.ts:23 `id: 'git_pull'`, push.ts:28 `id: 'git_push'`, reset.ts:29 `id: 'git_reset'`, and cherry-pick.ts:19 `id: 'git:cherry-pick'`. The wider grep for /id: 'git[_:]/ finds 22 git tool ids in src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/. Every one uses the underscore form (git_amend, git_blame, git_branch, git_checkout, git_commit, git_diff, git_fetch, git_log, git_merge, git_pull, git_push, git_rebase, git_remote, git_reset, git_revert, git_show, git_stage, git_stash, git_status, git_tag, git_worktree) except git:cherry-pick, which uses a colon. So the naming really is mixed.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The evidence confirms the premise: the four ids exist at the cited lines, and the naming is mixed (git:cherry-pick uses a colon, the other 21 git ids use an underscore). No edit is needed."}},{"claimId":"p10","ref":"s5/ac6","kind":"inventory","severity":"LOW","premise":"Existing gh tool ids gh_pr_create, gh_issue_comment, gh_release_delete and gh:project:item-add are registered.","evidence":"All four tool ids are defined in the cited anchor files: src/daemon/tools/builtins/gh/pr.ts:242 `id: 'gh_pr_create'`, src/daemon/tools/builtins/gh/issue.ts:191 `id: 'gh_issue_comment'`, src/daemon/tools/builtins/gh/release.ts:274 `id: 'gh_release_delete'`, src/daemon/tools/builtins/gh/project.ts:170 `id: 'gh:project:item-add'`. Each also uses its id in a title and in its fail/shellFail paths (e.g. pr.ts:268/294/309, project.ts:190/201/206/210). The full `id: 'gh[_:]` inventory (44 matches) shows the mixed naming the premise relies on: underscore ids in pr/issue/release/repo/actions and colon ids for the project item/field tools (project.ts:124-360). gh_pr_create is also listed in src/analyze/context/__tests__/tool-surface.test.ts:183. The evidence does not show the registry wiring itself, but the ids exist exactly as stated.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The premise matches the gathered evidence. All four ids exist at the cited anchors with the exact spellings given, including the colon-separated gh:project:item-add."}},{"claimId":"p11","ref":"s4/ac3","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"Destructive git operations (hard reset, force push) are already marked as needing approval in their tool definitions.","evidence":"The cited anchors resolve to approval-style titles, not to an approval flag. At src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/reset.ts:67 the line is `title: mode === 'hard' ? 'git:reset --hard (DESTRUCTIVE)' : 'git_reset'`, which marks a hard reset as DESTRUCTIVE in what looks like an approval descriptor. At src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/push.ts:71 the line is `title: protectedRef && (force || forceRaw) ? 'git:push (PROTECTED REF)' : 'git_push'`, so the special title applies only to a force push to a protected ref, not to every force push. The /requiresApproval/ grep stopped at its 50-match cap and shows only the cloud/aws and cloud/az tools, so the evidence never shows a `requiresApproval` declaration in git/reset.ts or git/push.ts. The premise is therefore only partly supported. A hard reset appears to be singled out. A force push is singled out only for protected refs, and the evidence cannot confirm that either tool actually requires approval.","action":"Read the requiresApproval field or predicate in git/reset.ts and git/push.ts to confirm that both tools declare approval, and to see which inputs trigger it. Then reword s4/ac3 to match: a hard reset is flagged as DESTRUCTIVE; a force push is flagged only when it targets a protected ref. If AC3 relies on every force push already needing approval, either add that gating to the story's scope or narrow the acceptance criterion.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The anchors show a DESTRUCTIVE title for `reset --hard` and a PROTECTED REF title for a force push to a protected ref only. The capped grep cannot show whether the git tools' requiresApproval is set, so the wording has to be confirmed or narrowed by a person rather than edited mechanically.","options":["Narrow the premise: hard reset is flagged DESTRUCTIVE (reset.ts:67); force push is flagged only for protected refs (push.ts:71). Force pushes to other refs are not covered today.","Keep the premise but add work to s4 so that every force push, not just a push to a protected ref, requires approval.","Verify requiresApproval in both git tool files first, then cite the actual predicate lines instead of the title lines."]}},{"claimId":"p12","ref":"s3","kind":"closed-union","severity":"MED","premise":"No question-and-answer approval exchange between the daemon and an attached client exists over IPC today.","evidence":"grep /approval\\.(request|answer|respond)/ returned 0 matches, which supports the claim that no approval question-and-answer IPC method exists. The other hits are only docs and comments. registerExternalGate appears in docs/plans/tools.md:547 ('render this via `channel.registerExternalGate`; the LLM tool-loop will') and in a comment at src/daemon/tools/executor.ts:10 ('the approval-gate dispatcher (channel.registerExternalGate, GateAction,'). GateAction appears in docs/plans/tools.md:60 (buildApprovalGate?) and in a comment at src/daemon/tools/types.ts:88. None of these is an IPC handler in the anchors src/daemon/index.ts or src/daemon/server.ts. But the comment at executor.ts:10 names an 'approval-gate dispatcher', and the evidence does not show whether that dispatcher is implemented or only planned. Neither anchor file was read, and the grep only checked the approval.* method names, so an approval exchange under another name (for example gate.* or question.*) is not ruled out.","action":"Confirm the premise directly. List the IPC method names registered in src/daemon/index.ts, and read the context around src/daemon/tools/executor.ts:10 to see whether the approval-gate dispatcher (channel.registerExternalGate) is implemented or only described. If that dispatcher does exist as an exchange over IPC, s3 should reuse or extend it rather than build a new one. If it does not, the premise holds as written.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The grep evidence supports the claim that no approval method exists, but the comment at executor.ts:10 points to a possible approval-gate dispatcher that the evidence does not settle. A human check decides whether s3 builds a new exchange or extends an existing one.","options":["Keep the premise as written once the index.ts handler registry shows no approval or gate methods.","Reword the premise: 'No approval question-and-answer IPC method exists; the tool executor has only an approval-gate dispatcher concept (channel.registerExternalGate / GateAction, executor.ts:10) that is not exposed over IPC.'","If the gate dispatcher is live and reachable over IPC, re-scope s3 to extend channel.registerExternalGate instead of adding a new exchange."]}},{"claimId":"p13","ref":"s6","kind":"external-contract","severity":"MED","premise":"Atlassian's acli exposes Jira issue, transition, comment, assignment and project operations; its command surface is unverified.","evidence":"All 27 /\\bacli\\b/ matches are in the project's own planning documents, not in source code or vendor docs. They are in docs/epics/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E2026102372024208/DEF.md (:33, :72, :79, :173, :183, :184, :187, :234, :242, :249, :280, :281), docs/epics/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E20261023b7a68147/DEF.md (:33, :62, :74, :82, :182, :192, :193, :196, :197, :227) and docs/standalone/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E202610230b8e0235/SPEC.md (:9, :13, :22, :56, :62). These lines state the requirement and the decision to wrap acli. None of them shows acli's real subcommands, its output format, how it is installed and signed in, or whether it supports transitions, comments, assignment and projects. The premise itself says the command surface is unverified. The newer DEF records this as a known gap: DEF.md:82 marks the assumption `low` with \"its command surface has not been checked yet\", S006:FR001 at DEF.md:62 requires acli's command list and output to be checked and recorded before the Jira tool families are designed, ac6 at DEF.md:197 says an operation acli does not offer must be named unsupported, and item a2 at DEF.md:227 asks for acli to be installed and its subcommands recorded. So the repo neither confirms nor contradicts the premise. It cannot be verified from this evidence, and the artifact already says so and plans a step to check it.","action":"Before the s6 LLD is written, check acli's real command surface by installing it and capturing `acli jira --help` plus the subcommand help for workitem/issue, transition, comment, assign and project, or by citing Atlassian's acli documentation. Record the result as S006:FR001 requires, and cite it in each Jira tool family. Mark any operation acli lacks as unsupported (ac6). Also record how a missing install differs from a missing sign-in, for ac2.","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"Whether acli offers these operations is a fact outside the repo. No evidence here supports a text edit, and the premise already says the surface is unverified. Settling it needs an external check, and possibly a scope decision if an operation turns out to be missing.","options":["Check acli's command surface (help output or Atlassian docs) and cite it in the s6 LLD as grounding for each Jira tool family (Story S006:FR001)","If acli lacks an operation (for example assignment or project listing), narrow s6 so that family is listed as unsupported under ac6 instead of approximated","Gate the s6 design.story on a recorded acli capability check (item a2), and keep the DEF assumption at `low` until that check is done"]}},{"claimId":"p14","ref":"s2/ac3","kind":"external-contract","severity":"MED","premise":"The gh CLI offers structured (JSON) output for many commands, while some CLI operations give only text requiring cheap-model conversion.","evidence":"The first half of the premise holds. The grep for /--json/ shows gh's structured output in use across the repo's design docs: team-signoff S008/LLD.md:266 and S008/PLAN.md:45 (`gh pr view --json headRefName,state`, `gh pr view --json state,mergedAt,reviews`), S009/LLD.md:105 and :330 (`gh issue view --json state,labels`), and understand-changes S001/identity-sources.md:7 (`gh auth status --json hosts`). The tool-registry DEF.md:245 (FR005) states the same premise. The second half is not covered by the evidence: no match shows a gh operation that gives only text, which is the half that justifies the cheap-model conversion path. None of the matches come from the anchor src/daemon/tools/builtins/gh. Every hit is a markdown design doc, so the evidence does not show how the existing gh builtins call gh. Most matches (claude/codex --json, --json-schema, qwen) are about other CLIs and say nothing on this premise.","action":"Back the text-only half with a concrete example: name at least one gh operation the s2 tool layer must wrap that has no --json output (for example `gh pr diff`, `gh pr checkout`, `gh auth status` without --json on older gh versions, or `gh run view --log`). Cite either a source file under src/daemon/tools/builtins/gh that parses gh text output today, or `gh <cmd> --help`. If no such operation is in scope, narrow AC3 so the model conversion path is not presented as required for gh.","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"gh's --json support is confirmed, but whether any in-scope gh operation is text-only is unverified, and only that half motivates the conversion path. The prescribed design still holds, since a conversion fallback costs nothing when unused, so this is MED, not HIGH. The evidence gives no value to auto-edit with.","options":["Name the specific text-only gh operations in scope (e.g. gh pr diff, gh run view --log), each with its gh --help or builtin source cited, and keep the conversion path.","Restrict the model conversion path in AC3 to git and Jira/acli operations that have been shown to be text-only, and require --json for every gh tool.","Keep AC3 as written, but mark the text-only half as an assumption to confirm during s2 build with a probe of each wrapped gh command."]}},{"claimId":"p15","kind":"cross-artifact","severity":"LOW","premise":"The cited spec docs/standalone/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E202610230b8e0235/SPEC.md exists and SPEC-0b8e02355ad3f578 records the brainstorm decisions.","evidence":"read docs/standalone/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E202610230b8e0235/SPEC.md:1 → FOUND: `<!-- insrc:artifact SPEC-0b8e02355ad3f578 -->`. The spec exists at the cited path and carries the cited artifact id. DEF.md:209 (E2026102372024208) and DEF.md:220 (E20261023b7a68147) both cite this SPEC path as source [[c1]]. The evidence shows that the file exists and that the id matches. It does not show the spec's content, so it cannot confirm which brainstorm decisions the spec records.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The spec exists at the cited path and its header names SPEC-0b8e02355ad3f578, so the reference is correct and no edit is needed."}},{"claimId":"p16","kind":"cross-artifact","severity":"LOW","premise":"Constraints k1-k4 trace to CLAUDE.md principles: no direct cloud REST, accuracy first, no parallel LLM calls, daemon owns all access via IPC.","evidence":"The evidence backs all four principles in CLAUDE.md. No direct cloud REST: CLAUDE.md:12 \"**No direct cloud REST calls from our process.** Cloud LLM access happens through the locally-installed `claude` and `codex` CLI binaries (via `CliProvider`)\". Accuracy first: CLAUDE.md:11 \"**Accuracy is primary; cost is the least priority.**\". No parallel LLM calls: CLAUDE.md:122 \"Never `Promise.all` anything that reaches an LLM provider (cloud or local Ollama embed / complete); always serial `for...of` with sequential awaits.\". Daemon owns access via IPC: CLAUDE.md:136 \"1. **Daemon owns all DB access** — CLI, MCP, and the IDE workbench communicate via IPC only.\". AGENTS.md has the same text at lines 11, 12, 122 and 136.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"All four constraints trace to text that exists in CLAUDE.md, so no fix is needed. One wording point: CLAUDE.md:136 says the daemon owns all DB access, which is narrower than the premise's 'all access', but the IPC-only rule the premise relies on is the same."}},{"claimId":"p17","kind":"ordering","severity":"MED","premise":"s4, s5 and s6 depend on s2 and s3; s7 depends on s1-s5 but not s6.","evidence":"No probes were run for this premise, so there is no ripgrep match or path:line read to confirm or contradict the claimed story ordering (s4/s5/s6 depend on s2+s3; s7 depends on s1-s5 but not s6). The premise cannot be verified from the evidence given.","action":"Check the ordering against the Define/HLD story dependency list (each story's dependsOn). Confirm that s4, s5 and s6 each list s2 and s3, and that s7 lists s1-s5 and leaves out s6. If they don't match, correct the premise or the story dependencies.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The premise is an ordering claim with no gathered evidence. It needs a human to check it against the artifact's story dependency declarations before any edit.","options":["Keep the premise as written once the Define/HLD story dependsOn entries are confirmed to match","Add s6 to s7's dependencies if s7 actually consumes something s6 produces","Correct the s4/s5/s6 dependency sets if any of them does not actually need both s2 and s3"]}},{"claimId":"p18","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"The git runner inside the editor plugin's process lives outside the daemon/backend and is out of scope for migration.","evidence":"The evidence does not show a production git runner inside the editor plugin. Every vscode-plugin hit is in a test file or a document that quotes one: vscode-plugin/src/chat/__tests__/docs-review-panel.test.ts:1501, :3567, :4023 and :5648 call `execFileSync('git', …)` directly, or a local `git(...)` helper. Other hits mention vscode-plugin/src/extension.ts:633 (a CR note on tabIcon, nothing to do with git). The evidence does support that the plugin is a separate source tree (vscode-plugin/) from the backend (src/). The revised DEF agrees with the non-goal: docs/epics/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E20261023b7a68147/DEF.md:212 scopes the build check to 'a new direct git or gh call appears in the backend outside the registry'. The earlier DEF (E2026102372024208/DEF.md:202) had no 'in the backend' qualifier. So the scope boundary is consistent, but the premise names a specific plugin runner, and the 50-match cap cut the grep off before any such runner turned up. The premise is unverifiable from this evidence. It is also not build-breaking: a non-goal that excludes plugin code does not change what gets built in src/.","action":"Name the plugin's git runner by file:line (for example, the module under vscode-plugin/src that spawns git at runtime), or reword the non-goal to exclude all git calls under vscode-plugin/, tests included. Make sure the ac2 build check is scoped to src/ so plugin code and test fixtures do not trip it.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The scope boundary (backend src/ vs the vscode-plugin/ tree) is supported, and DEF ac2 already says 'in the backend'. What is missing is a concrete anchor for the plugin's git runner. The evidence shows only test-file git calls in vscode-plugin, so the premise should either cite the runtime runner or be reworded as a tree-level exclusion.","options":["Reword the non-goal: 'Git and gh calls under vscode-plugin/ (the editor plugin's own process, including its tests) are out of scope; the registry cutover and its build check cover src/ only.'","Keep the wording and add a citation to the plugin module that actually runs git at runtime (to be confirmed by a targeted grep under vscode-plugin/src excluding __tests__).","Bring plugin git calls into scope if a later story needs one registry across processes (needs a design decision)."]}}],"counts":{"high":0,"med":11,"low":7},"reviewedAt":"2026-10-23T08:45:55.619Z","model":"cli-claude:opus","reviewedBy":"daemon"},"resolutions":{}}}}}
