d 0 matches, supporting the absence of Jira tools. AGENTS.md:19 and design/analyze-framework.md:29 list the built-in tool categories without Jira.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The targeted absence probe supports the premise; no correction is needed."}},{"claimId":"p07","ref":"1","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"Workflow callers currently obtain checked result types from their direct git and GitHub wrappers rather than from registry result declarations.","evidence":"Unverifiable from the supplied evidence: all three searches are truncated and show documentation or recorded measurements, with no current src/workflow definitions or caller reads. docs/plans/tool-registry.md:24 and :52 describe wrapper callers, but do not establish their current result typing. docs/daemon.md:1357 documents schema-derived callTool result types, but does not show whether workflow callers use them. The evidence therefore establishes neither the claimed current typing source nor its exclusion of registry declarations.","action":"Gather current source definitions and representative workflow caller reads for runGit, commitAndPushArtifacts and github.ts, alongside registry result declarations and their workflow usage, before retaining this premise.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The supplied documentation cannot establish current caller typing; no evidence-derived mechanical correction is safe.","options":["Re-run probes scoped to src/workflow and src/daemon/tools, including wrapper return signatures and caller imports, then reassess the premise.","Mark the current typing source as unverified until source reads establish whether callers derive results from wrappers or registry declarations."]}},{"claimId":"p08","ref":"1","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"Tools currently declare whether they require approval.","evidence":"UNVERIFIABLE against source: the truncated search supplies no matches or reads under src/daemon/tools. docs/daemon.md:1385 describes an approval field on tool definitions, and docs/epics/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E20261023b7a68147/HLD.md:34 describes requiresApproval flags, but these documentation claims do not establish the current implementation.","action":"Gather the tool-definition type and representative builtin declarations from source to verify how tools declare approval requirements.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The evidence supports documentation claims but provides no source grounding for the anchored premise; no mechanical correction is justified.","options":["Verify the premise using source reads of the tool-definition type and builtin approval declarations.","Qualify the premise as a documented claim pending source verification."]}},{"claimId":"p09","ref":"1","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"The executor currently executes tools requiring approval as though approval had already been granted.","evidence":"Unverifiable: the supplied grep output is truncated at 50 matches and contains no executor source matches or reads under src/daemon/tools. Matches such as AGENTS.md:228 and docs/daemon.md:1345 concern workflow approval or test records, and do not establish whether the tool executor bypasses required approval.","action":"Gather scoped matches and source reads from src/daemon/tools showing approval metadata, the executor's approval checks, and the path to tool invocation; reassess the premise against that evidence.","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The supplied evidence neither confirms nor contradicts the executor behavior, so no evidence-derived artifact correction is safe.","options":["Re-probe src/daemon/tools for approval flags and read the executor branches that consume them.","Defer the behavioral claim until evidence traces an approval-required tool through its execution path."]}},{"claimId":"p10","ref":"1","kind":"closed-union","severity":"MED","premise":"No existing shared execution point enforces tracker refresh after every backend pull or push.","evidence":"Unverifiable from the supplied evidence: both git-operation and hook searches were truncated at 50 matches and provide no source reads under src/daemon/tools, src/workflow, or src/cli. docs/daemon.md:1407 describes a shared registerToolHook execution mechanism, but does not establish that tracker refresh is registered for both pull and push. The tracker refresh/sync probe returned zero matches; this does not prove absence given the documented hook mechanism and lack of source coverage.","action":"Gather source evidence for shared tool execution, hook registrations, and backend pull/push callers before asserting that no shared execution point enforces refresh.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The supplied documentation identifies a shared hook mechanism, but the evidence cannot establish whether tracker refresh is wired into it.","options":["Inspect the shared hook execution and registrations, then retain or correct the premise based on source evidence.","Reword the premise to state that enforcement of tracker refresh after every backend pull or push remains unverified."]}},{"claimId":"p11","ref":"3","kind":"external-contract","severity":"MED","premise":"Git commands launched independently in a terminal or IDE outside the daemon do not pass through the daemon tool executor.","evidence":"Unverifiable from the supplied evidence. docs/daemon.md:1409 describes executeTool's success exits, and docs/epics/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E20261023b7a68147/HLD.md:252 describes wrappers calling executeTool. Neither establishes how independently launched terminal or IDE Git commands execute. Both searches are truncated and supply no source reads from src/daemon/tools or src/daemon/server.ts demonstrating this boundary.","action":"Gather source evidence establishing the executor's invocation boundary and whether external Git commands have any interception path before treating this premise as verified.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The evidence neither confirms nor contradicts the claimed external execution boundary; no mechanical correction is supported.","options":["Retain the premise as an explicit assumption pending targeted source evidence.","Provide executor and IPC dispatch source reads plus evidence addressing external Git interception, then reassess."]}},{"claimId":"p12","ref":"3","kind":"external-contract","severity":"MED","premise":"The editor plugin has a git runner in its own process outside this backend's migration scope.","evidence":"The supplied grep /(?i)(editor|plugin|workbench).*git|git.*(editor|plugin|workbench)/ returned 0 matches. No read lines establish an editor-plugin git runner, its process boundary, or its exclusion from migration scope; the premise is unverifiable.","action":"Supply evidence identifying the editor plugin's git runner and process boundary, or qualify the scope exclusion as an unverified assumption.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The empty evidence neither confirms nor contradicts the external contract, so no evidence-derived factual replacement is safe.","options":["Provide source evidence for the editor plugin's git runner, process boundary, and migration exclusion.","Reword the premise as an unverified assumption pending external-contract verification."]}},{"claimId":"p13","ref":"4/a1","kind":"external-contract","severity":"LOW","premise":"Atlassian acli can be installed and authenticated on machines that will use the Jira tools.","evidence":"docs/plans/tool-registry.md:32 records a probe using acli 1.3.39-stable, signed in via OAuth to insors.atlassian.net, demonstrating an installed and authenticated instance. Line 44 records the signed-out auth-status response and directs users to `acli jira auth login`, supporting the authentication path. This confirms feasibility, though not installation on every target machine.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The recorded probe supports the installation and authentication premise; no artifact correction is needed."}},{"claimId":"p14","ref":"4/a1","kind":"external-contract","severity":"MED","premise":"Atlassian acli provides the Jira issue transition, comment, assignment and project operations required by the Epic.","evidence":"The supplied matches contain requirements and assumptions, not verified acli command output. docs/epics/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E20261023b7a68147/DEF.md:82 explicitly says its command surface has not been checked; :62 and :241 require checking and recording it before Jira design. :192 scopes coverage to supported operations, and :197 requires unsupported operations to be named. HLD.md:173 lists intended families but supplies no external-contract verification. Thus the premise is unverifiable; no concrete build-breaking contradiction is established.","action":"Record acli's actual command help and representative output for transitions, comments, assignment and projects, then reconcile the required families and unsupported operations before designing the Jira tools.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The evidence supports treating capability coverage as an unchecked assumption, but does not establish which operations acli actually supports.","options":["Reword the premise as an unchecked assumption gated on recording acli command help and output before Jira design.","Supply captured acli capability evidence for each required family, then retain or narrow the premise accordingly."]}},{"claimId":"p15","ref":"4/a2","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"The executor has a single entry point where hooks and result validation can be added without changing agent tool invocation.","evidence":"docs/daemon.md:1360 describes result validation in executeTool, and docs/daemon.md:1409 describes hooks at its two success exits. These support the proposed extension point, but both capped searches return documentation rather than source matches or reads establishing executeTool's implementation and agent invocation path. The single-entry-point and unchanged-invocation claims therefore remain unverifiable; no concrete build-breaking contradiction is shown.","action":"Gather source reads of executeTool and its agent call sites to verify that validation and hooks can run centrally without changing invocation.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The supplied documentation supports the concept but does not establish the source-level routing claim.","options":["Verify the premise with source reads of the executor and agent dispatch call sites.","Qualify the premise as documented architecture pending source verification."]}},{"claimId":"p16","ref":"4/a3","kind":"external-contract","severity":"MED","premise":"A cheap model can convert unstructured CLI output into the declared result shape often enough to make the conversion useful.","evidence":"The supplied grep for CLI/tool conversion, normalization, or structuring returned 0 matches. No supplied evidence establishes a cheap model's conversion accuracy or usefulness; the premise is unverifiable.","action":"Validate conversion against representative CLI outputs with a defined success threshold, or explicitly treat the premise as an unvalidated assumption.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"An empty search result neither confirms nor contradicts the claimed model capability. Establishing usefulness requires empirical evidence and an agreed success criterion.","options":["Run a representative conversion evaluation and define the minimum acceptable success rate.","Reword the premise as an unvalidated assumption pending evaluation."]}},{"claimId":"p17","ref":"4/a3","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"Schema validation can reject structurally invalid converted output without detecting every incorrect value that satisfies the schema.","evidence":"docs/daemon.md:1360 states that output-schema mismatches produce schema-mismatch failures with data dropped. This supports structural rejection. However, both grep results are truncated and provide no source reads or conversion examples establishing that semantically incorrect, schema-conforming values pass validation. The full premise is therefore unverifiable from the supplied evidence.","action":"Supply validator/conversion source reads and an example demonstrating a schema-conforming but incorrect converted value.","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The evidence supports structural validation but does not establish the claimed semantic limitation for converted output.","options":["Provide source reads and a test showing that an incorrect converted value satisfying the schema passes validation.","Narrow the premise to the documented rejection of output-schema mismatches."]}},{"claimId":"p18","ref":"4/a4","kind":"external-contract","severity":"MED","premise":"Interactive daemon use normally has an attached terminal, editor or chat client with a connection capable of carrying approval questions and answers.","evidence":"AGENTS.md:131 confirms JSON-RPC over a Unix socket. README.md:221–223 describes interactive workflow approvals, and AGENTS.md:260 describes explicit in-chat approval. These establish client and approval surfaces, but the truncated matches contain no source reads from src/daemon/server.ts or src/cli establishing an attached client or a connection capable of carrying approval questions and answers. The premise is therefore unverifiable; no concrete build-breaking contradiction is established.","action":"Validate the approval request/response transport and client attachment lifecycle, or qualify the premise as an assumption requiring capability detection.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The evidence supports existing approval surfaces but does not establish the claimed runtime connection capability or normal attachment.","options":["Retain the premise pending source evidence of client attachment and bidirectional approval transport.","Reword it as an assumption and require runtime detection of an approval-capable client before requesting approval."]}},{"claimId":"p19","ref":"4/a4","kind":"closed-union","severity":"MED","premise":"The current daemon-client protocol has no approval question-and-answer exchange.","evidence":"Unverifiable: the approval/approve/permission probe reports zero matches, but no reads of src/daemon/server.ts or src/shared/types.ts establish the protocol's complete surface. The notification/JSON-RPC probe is truncated and primarily returns documentation. S003/PLAN.md:133 describes approval.subscribe and approval.answer, while S003/CR.md:24 references src/daemon/approval-broker.ts:52; these documentation matches do not establish current implementation or reachability.","action":"Verify the daemon's registered handlers and protocol types, including any approval subscription and answer handlers, before asserting that no approval question-and-answer exchange exists.","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The supplied evidence does not establish either the absence or implementation of this exchange, so no evidence-derived replacement is safe.","options":["Gather source reads covering protocol types, handler registration, and approval exchange implementation, then reassess the premise.","Qualify the premise as unverified pending inspection of the current daemon-client protocol."]}},{"claimId":"p20","ref":"k1","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"CLAUDE.md requires cloud LLM access through local CLI sessions and prohibits direct cloud REST providers and stored cloud credentials.","evidence":"CLAUDE.md:12 explicitly requires locally installed claude/codex binaries via CliProvider, retains auth in CLI OAuth sessions, and prohibits direct cloud REST providers. CLAUDE.md:41 states cloud auth is delegated to the CLI OAuth session with no API keys stored on this side; :137 reiterates CLI-only cloud access and no direct REST. The Anthropic API-key matches in src/daemon/index.ts:1468–1472 do not contradict what CLAUDE.md requires.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The premise describes the documented requirement, which the supplied CLAUDE.md matches confirm."}},{"claimId":"p21","ref":"k2","kind":"cross-artifact","severity":"MED","premise":"Constraint k2 attributes the requirement to reject schema-invalid tool results to the accuracy-first principle in CLAUDE.md.","evidence":"The only supplied read of CLAUDE.md is line 1, its title; it does not establish the accuracy-first principle or k2's attribution. docs/daemon.md:1360 explicitly documents rejecting schema-invalid ToolResult.data as schema-mismatch, but does not establish the claimed attribution to CLAUDE.md. Neither k2 nor [[c2]] was supplied for comparison.","action":"Verify k2 and [[c2]] against the relevant CLAUDE.md principle, or cite docs/daemon.md:1360 for the explicit result-validation requirement.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The requirement has supporting evidence, but its claimed cross-artifact attribution is unverifiable and the artifact text needed for an exact edit is absent.","options":["Supply the relevant CLAUDE.md passage and k2/[[c2]] text to verify the attribution.","Reword k2 to cite docs/daemon.md:1360 for the explicit rejection behavior, treating accuracy-first as rationale only."]}},{"claimId":"p22","ref":"k3","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"CLAUDE.md prohibits parallel calls reaching any LLM provider, including local embedding and completion calls.","evidence":"CLAUDE.md:122 explicitly states: “Never `Promise.all` anything that reaches an LLM provider (cloud or local Ollama embed / complete); always serial `for...of` with sequential awaits.” This directly confirms the premise, including local embedding and completion calls.","action":"none — verified